{"id":19,"course_id":8,"title":"Config, Secrets, Requests, and Probes","summary":"Learn the basic knobs that make workloads configurable, schedulable, and safe to receive traffic.","sequence":4,"body_simplified":"## Runtime contract\nKubernetes separates image, configuration, scheduling needs, and health checks. Beginners should learn these early because many incidents come from missing or confused settings.\n- ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive configuration.\n- Secrets hold sensitive values but still need RBAC, encryption at rest, and rotation.\n- Resource requests tell the scheduler what CPU and memory the Pod needs.\n- Readiness controls traffic. Liveness controls restart. Startup protects slow boot.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\n$ kubectl get cm,secret -n payments\n$ kubectl top pod -n payments\n$ kubectl get events -n payments --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nLook for missing config keys, wrong secret names, Pending Pods due to insufficient resources, failed readiness probes, and OOMKilled restarts.\n\n## Tradeoff\nLiveness probes should be conservative. A bad liveness probe can turn a slow dependency into a restart storm.","body_traditional":"## Runtime contract\nKubernetes separates image, configuration, scheduling needs, and health checks. Beginners should learn these early because many incidents come from missing or confused settings.\n- ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive configuration.\n- Secrets hold sensitive values but still need RBAC, encryption at rest, and rotation.\n- Resource requests tell the scheduler what CPU and memory the Pod needs.\n- Readiness controls traffic. Liveness controls restart. Startup protects slow boot.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\n$ kubectl get cm,secret -n payments\n$ kubectl top pod -n payments\n$ kubectl get events -n payments --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nLook for missing config keys, wrong secret names, Pending Pods due to insufficient resources, failed readiness probes, and OOMKilled restarts.\n\n## Tradeoff\nLiveness probes should be conservative. A bad liveness probe can turn a slow dependency into a restart storm.","pinyin":"Scenario: Pods start but never receive traffic.\nChecklist:\n- Read readiness probe failures in Pod events.\n- Check application logs for missing config.\n- Confirm requests fit available node capacity.\n- Do not loosen probes until you know whether the app can serve users.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\n$ kubectl logs checkout-abc123 -n payments --since=10m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-kubernetes-fundamentals","course_category":"Kubernetes","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":66,"simplified":"ConfigMap","traditional":"ConfigMap","pinyin":"Configuration","definition":"A Kubernetes object for non-sensitive runtime configuration."},{"id":67,"simplified":"Secret","traditional":"Secret","pinyin":"Sensitive config","definition":"A Kubernetes object for sensitive values that still needs access control and rotation."},{"id":68,"simplified":"Request","traditional":"Request","pinyin":"Scheduling","definition":"The CPU or memory amount used by the scheduler to place a Pod."},{"id":69,"simplified":"Readiness probe","traditional":"Readiness probe","pinyin":"Traffic gate","definition":"A check that decides whether a Pod should receive Service traffic."},{"id":70,"simplified":"Liveness probe","traditional":"Liveness probe","pinyin":"Restart gate","definition":"A check that restarts a container when restart is likely to help."}],"flashcards":[{"id":40,"lesson_id":19,"prompt":"What is the difference between readiness and liveness?","answer":"Readiness controls traffic; liveness controls restarts.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":41,"lesson_id":19,"prompt":"Are Kubernetes Secrets automatically a complete security solution?","answer":"No. They still need RBAC, encryption at rest, audit, and rotation.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":42,"lesson_id":19,"prompt":"Why do resource requests matter?","answer":"The scheduler uses requests to place Pods on nodes with enough capacity.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
